       "say": "**Trace a program by hand.** Run it in your head, line by line, and keep a note of what each box holds: line 1, x is 5. Line 2, y is x × 2 = 10. Line 3, `x = x + 1` reads as \"take what is in x, add 1, put it back in x\": x becomes 6 (while y stays 10, because y was calculated earlier). Line 4, z = x + y = 16. The prints show exactly that. The sentence `x = x + 1` looks strange as maths (no number equals itself plus one) because `=` in Python is **not** \"equals\": it means \"put the right-hand side into the box on the left\". Tracing on paper is how professionals understand code they did not write, and how they find bugs.",
