       "say": "**Errors are messages, not failures.** When Python cannot follow a line it stops and tells you the kind of problem and where. Five common ones, each caught and printed here: **NameError**, \"name 'total_price' is not defined\" (you used a box that does not exist: a typo, or you never made it); **TypeError**, \"can only concatenate str (not \"int\") to str\" (you tried to add text and a number); **ZeroDivisionError** (dividing by zero); **IndexError**, \"list index out of range\" (asking for the sixth item of a three-item list); **ValueError**, \"invalid literal for int() with base 10: 'hello'\" (the right type of thing, but a value that does not make sense, such as turning \"hello\" into a whole number). Read the **last line** of an error first: it names the problem in plain words. Programmers see these constantly; the skill is reading them."
