  {
   "id": "fn-dicts",
   "icon": "📚",
   "title": "Functions: Dictionaries, sets and tuples",
   "sub": "A dictionary stores label: value pairs in curly brackets, like a two-column lookup table.",
   "simple": "A **dictionary** stores label: value pairs in curly brackets, like a two-column lookup table. A **set** keeps only unique items. A **tuple** is a fixed list.",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "explorer",
     "echo": true,
     "tables": true,
     "cumulative": true,
     "starter": "",
     "items": [
      {
       "name": "{label: value, ...}",
       "say": "Makes a dictionary. The labels (keys) are usually text; each label points at a value. It is a lookup table. **Watch for:** Each label can appear only once. Writing the same label twice keeps the last value. **In Excel:** a two-column lookup table (VLOOKUP)",
       "code": "person = {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"city\": \"Pune\", \"score\": 87}\nprint(person)\nprint(len(person))"
      },
      {
       "name": "dict[label]",
       "say": "Looks up the value for a label. Assign to change it or to add a new label. **Watch for:** A label that does not exist is a `KeyError`. Use `.get` to be safe. **In Excel:** =VLOOKUP(label, table, 2, 0)",
       "code": "print(person[\"city\"])\nperson[\"score\"] = 91\nperson[\"age\"] = 21\nprint(person)"
      },
      {
       "name": "dict.get(label, default)",
       "say": "Looks up a label but, if it is missing, gives you a default instead of an error. **Watch for:** Without the default you get `None`. **In Excel:** =IFERROR(VLOOKUP(...), \"not given\")",
       "code": "print(person.get(\"city\"))\nprint(person.get(\"phone\"))\nprint(person.get(\"phone\", \"not given\"))"
      },
      {
       "name": "dict.keys() dict.values() dict.items()",
       "say": "The labels, the values, and the (label, value) pairs. Wrap in `list(...)` to see them. `items()` is the one you loop over. **Watch for:** A plain `for x in person` loops over the labels only.",
       "code": "print(list(person.keys()))\nprint(list(person.values()))\nfor label, value in person.items():\n    print(label, \"->\", value)"
      },
      {
       "name": "dict.update(other) dict.pop(label) del dict[label]",
       "say": "Merge another dictionary in; remove a label and get its value back; delete a label. **Watch for:** Removing a missing label is a KeyError; `pop(label, None)` is the safe form.",
       "code": "person.update({\"city\": \"Goa\", \"team\": \"A\"})\nprint(person)\nage = person.pop(\"age\")\nprint(age)\ndel person[\"team\"]\nprint(person)"
      },
      {
       "name": "label in dict",
       "say": "True if the label exists. It checks labels, not values. **In Excel:** =ISNUMBER(MATCH(label, range, 0))",
       "code": "print(\"city\" in person)\nprint(\"Goa\" in person)\nprint(\"Goa\" in person.values())"
      },
      {
       "name": "counting with a dictionary",
       "say": "The classic pattern: go through a list and tally how many times each thing appears. `.get(x, 0) + 1` starts a new tally at zero. **Watch for:** This is exactly what `Counter` does for you (section 8). **In Excel:** a pivot table: count by label",
       "code": "sales = [\"tea\", \"coffee\", \"tea\", \"tea\", \"coffee\", \"juice\"]\ncounts = {}\nfor item in sales:\n    counts[item] = counts.get(item, 0) + 1\nprint(counts)\nprint(sorted(counts.items(), key=lambda p: p[1], reverse=True))"
      },
      {
       "name": "dict(zip(keys, values))",
       "say": "Builds a dictionary from two lists, pairing them up. **Watch for:** `max(week, key=week.get)` returns the label with the largest value.",
       "code": "labels = [\"mon\", \"tue\", \"wed\"]\nsales = [120, 90, 150]\nweek = dict(zip(labels, sales))\nprint(week)\nprint(max(week, key=week.get))"
      },
      {
       "name": "set(list) {a, b}",
       "say": "A **set** keeps each value once and ignores order. Turning a list into a set removes duplicates. **Watch for:** `{}` on its own is an empty dictionary; an empty set is `set()`. **In Excel:** Remove Duplicates",
       "code": "cities = [\"Pune\", \"Delhi\", \"Pune\", \"Goa\", \"Delhi\"]\nunique = set(cities)\nprint(unique)\nprint(len(unique))\nprint(sorted(unique))"
      },
      {
       "name": "a | b a & b a - b (set maths)",
       "say": "Union (everything), intersection (in both) and difference (in the first only). **Watch for:** Handy for \"who bought in both months\", \"who is new\". **In Excel:** comparing two columns",
       "code": "bought_jan = {\"asha\", \"ben\", \"chen\"}\nbought_feb = {\"ben\", \"chen\", \"dara\"}\nprint(bought_jan | bought_feb)\nprint(bought_jan & bought_feb)\nprint(bought_jan - bought_feb)"
      },
      {
       "name": "set.add(x) set.remove(x)",
       "say": "Add an item (ignored if present) or remove one.",
       "code": "seen = set()\nseen.add(\"asha\")\nseen.add(\"asha\")\nseen.add(\"ben\")\nprint(seen)\nseen.remove(\"ben\")\nprint(seen)"
      },
      {
       "name": "(a, b) tuple(list)",
       "say": "A **tuple** is a list that cannot be changed, written in round brackets. Used for pairs like coordinates or (name, score). You can unpack it. **Watch for:** A tuple of one item needs a trailing comma: `(5,)`.",
       "code": "point = (3, 4)\nx, y = point\nprint(point[0], x, y)\nrecord = (\"Asha\", 21, \"Pune\")\nname, age, city = record\nprint(name, age, city)"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "fn-flow",
   "icon": "📚",
   "title": "Functions: Decisions and loops",
   "sub": "Programs decide and repeat.",
   "simple": "Programs decide and repeat. A line ending in a **colon** opens a block; the **indented** lines under it belong to it.",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "explorer",
     "echo": true,
     "tables": true,
     "cumulative": true,
     "starter": "",
     "items": [
      {
       "name": "if / elif / else",
       "say": "Do something only when a test is True. `elif` (\"else if\") adds another test; `else` catches everything left. **Watch for:** Python tests from the top and stops at the first True one. Do not forget the colons or the 4-space indent. **In Excel:** =IF(B2>=90,\"A\",IF(B2>=60,\"B\",\"C\"))",
       "code": "score = 72\nif score >= 90:\n    grade = \"A\"\nelif score >= 60:\n    grade = \"B\"\nelse:\n    grade = \"C\"\nprint(grade)"
      },
      {
       "name": "x if test else y",
       "say": "A one-line choice between two values. **Watch for:** Good for short choices; use a normal if for anything longer. **In Excel:** =IF(test, x, y)",
       "code": "age = 20\nstatus = \"adult\" if age >= 18 else \"minor\"\nprint(status)\nprices = [120, 40, 90]\nprint([\"cheap\" if p < 100 else \"dear\" for p in prices])"
      },
      {
       "name": "for item in list:",
       "say": "Repeat a block once for each item. The name after `for` holds the current item. **Watch for:** The loop name (`p`) only exists for the loop; pick a meaningful one. **In Excel:** filling a formula down a column",
       "code": "prices = [100, 250, 80]\ntotal = 0\nfor p in prices:\n    gst = p * 0.18\n    total = total + gst\n    print(p, gst)\nprint(\"Total GST:\", total)"
      },
      {
       "name": "for i in range(n):",
       "say": "Repeat a block a set number of times; `i` counts 0, 1, 2, ... **Watch for:** range(3) gives 0, 1, 2, so the block runs 3 times.",
       "code": "for i in range(3):\n    print(\"round\", i)\nfor year in range(1, 4):\n    print(year, round(1000 * 1.08 ** year, 2))"
      },
      {
       "name": "while condition:",
       "say": "Repeat as long as a test stays True. Make sure something inside the block changes the test, or it never ends. **Watch for:** A loop that never finishes is called an infinite loop; the Stop button on this site ends it.",
       "code": "balance = 1000\nyears = 0\nwhile balance < 2000:\n    balance = balance * 1.10\n    years += 1\nprint(years, round(balance, 2))"
      },
      {
       "name": "break continue pass",
       "say": "`break` leaves the loop completely. `continue` skips to the next item. `pass` does nothing (a placeholder).",
       "code": "for n in range(1, 10):\n    if n % 2 == 0:\n        continue\n    if n > 7:\n        break\n    print(n)"
      },
      {
       "name": "[expression for item in list]",
       "say": "A **list comprehension**: build a new list from an old one in one line. Read it as \"give me this, for each item\". **Watch for:** The part after `if` filters; the part at the start transforms. **In Excel:** a new column with a formula",
       "code": "prices = [100, 250, 80]\nwith_gst = [p * 1.18 for p in prices]\nprint(with_gst)\nbig = [p for p in prices if p > 90]\nprint(big)"
      },
      {
       "name": "{k: v for ...} {x for ...}",
       "say": "The same one-line idea for dictionaries and sets.",
       "code": "names = [\"Asha\", \"Ben\", \"Chen\"]\nlengths = {n: len(n) for n in names}\nprint(lengths)\nletters = {n[0] for n in names}\nprint(sorted(letters))"
      },
      {
       "name": "any(...) all(...)",
       "say": "Is at least one (`any`) or every (`all`) item True? Feed them a comprehension. **Watch for:** True counts as 1, so `sum(test for item in list)` counts how many pass. **In Excel:** =COUNTIF(range, \">=60\")",
       "code": "marks = [70, 85, 40, 90]\nprint(any(m < 50 for m in marks))\nprint(all(m >= 40 for m in marks))\nprint(sum(m >= 60 for m in marks))"
      },
      {
       "name": "nested loops",
       "say": "A loop inside a loop: for every row, go through every column. Indentation shows the nesting. **Watch for:** Two levels of 100 items means 10,000 runs; keep nesting small for big data (later modules show faster ways). **In Excel:** a multiplication table",
       "code": "for r in range(1, 4):\n    row = []\n    for c in range(1, 4):\n        row.append(r * c)\n    print(row)"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "fn-funcs",
   "icon": "📚",
   "title": "Functions: Functions you write",
   "sub": "A function is a recipe with a name.",
   "simple": "A function is a recipe with a name. You define it once and call it as often as you like.",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "explorer",
     "echo": true,
     "tables": true,
     "cumulative": true,
     "starter": "",
     "items": [
      {
       "name": "def name(parameters): ... return value",
       "say": "Defines a function. Parameters are names for the inputs; `return` hands the answer back. **Watch for:** Defining does not run it. The code inside runs only when you call it: `with_gst(100)`. Return, not print, if you want to store the answer. **In Excel:** a reusable formula",
       "code": "def with_gst(price):\n    return price * 1.18\n\nprint(with_gst(100))\nbill = with_gst(250)\nprint(bill)"
      },
      {
       "name": "default values def f(a, b=10)",
       "say": "Give a parameter a default so the caller may leave it out. **Watch for:** Parameters with defaults must come after those without.",
       "code": "def with_gst(price, rate=0.18):\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n\nprint(with_gst(100))\nprint(with_gst(100, 0.05))\nprint(with_gst(100, rate=0.28))"
      },
      {
       "name": "f(name=value) (keyword arguments)",
       "say": "Pass an argument by naming it. Makes calls self-explanatory and lets you skip optional ones.",
       "code": "def loan(amount, years=5, rate=0.09):\n    return round(amount * (1 + rate) ** years, 2)\n\nprint(loan(1000))\nprint(loan(1000, rate=0.07))\nprint(loan(amount=500, years=2))"
      },
      {
       "name": "return a, b (several results)",
       "say": "A function can return several values at once; they arrive as a tuple you can unpack.",
       "code": "def stats(values):\n    return min(values), max(values), sum(values) / len(values)\n\nlow, high, mean = stats([70, 85, 90])\nprint(low, high, mean)"
      },
      {
       "name": "lambda x: expression",
       "say": "A tiny nameless function in one line, mostly used as a `key` for sorting or as a quick transformation. **Watch for:** For anything longer than one expression use a normal `def`.",
       "code": "square = lambda x: x * x\nprint(square(7))\nitems = [(\"tea\", 30), (\"coffee\", 50), (\"juice\", 40)]\nprint(sorted(items, key=lambda it: it[1], reverse=True))"
      },
      {
       "name": "map(function, list) filter(function, list)",
       "say": "`map` applies a function to every item; `filter` keeps items where the function says True. Wrap in `list()`. Comprehensions often read better.",
       "code": "prices = [100, 250, 80]\nprint(list(map(lambda p: p * 2, prices)))\nprint(list(filter(lambda p: p > 90, prices)))\nprint([p * 2 for p in prices])"
      },
      {
       "name": "args and kwargs",
       "say": "Let a function accept any number of arguments. `*args` collects extra positional values into a tuple; `**kwargs` collects extra named values into a dictionary. **Watch for:** You will see these in library code (`plt.plot(*args, **kwargs)`); you need them rarely when writing your own.",
       "code": "def total(*numbers):\n    return sum(numbers)\n\nprint(total(1, 2, 3, 4))\n\ndef describe(**details):\n    return details\n\nprint(describe(name=\"Asha\", city=\"Pune\"))"
      },
      {
       "name": "docstring \"\"\"explains the function\"\"\"",
       "say": "A text right under `def` that says what the function does. `help(f)` shows it. Good habit. **In Excel:** a note on a formula",
       "code": "def with_gst(price, rate=0.18):\n    \"\"\"Return the price including GST (default 18%).\"\"\"\n    return price * (1 + rate)\n\nprint(with_gst.__doc__)"
      },
      {
       "name": "local and global names",
       "say": "Names created inside a function exist only inside it. A function can read names from outside, but assigning to a name inside creates a new local one. **Watch for:** Pass values in as parameters instead of relying on outside names; it is safer.",
       "code": "rate = 0.18\n\ndef gst(price):\n    amount = price * rate\n    return amount\n\nprint(gst(100))\ntry:\n    print(amount)\nexcept NameError:\n    print(\"amount only exists inside gst()\")"
      },
      {
       "name": "a function that calls itself (recursion)",
       "say": "A function may call itself on a smaller problem until a simple case stops it. Rarely needed in data work, but good to recognise. **Watch for:** Always include the stopping case, or it never ends.",
       "code": "def factorial(n):\n    if n <= 1:\n        return 1\n    return n * factorial(n - 1)\n\nprint(factorial(5))"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  },
  {
   "id": "fn-lib",
   "icon": "📚",
   "title": "Functions: Files, errors, imports and the standard library",
   "sub": "Real work reads files, copes with bad input and borrows tools from the standard library (the toolboxes that ship with Python).",
   "simple": "Real work reads files, copes with bad input and borrows tools from the standard library (the toolboxes that ship with Python).",
   "blocks": [
    {
     "t": "explorer",
     "echo": true,
     "tables": true,
     "cumulative": true,
     "starter": "",
     "items": [
      {
       "name": "open(path, \"w\") file.write(text)",
       "say": "Create or replace a file and write text to it. Always use `with`, which closes the file for you at the end of the indented block. **Watch for:** Mode `\"w\"` **erases** an existing file. Use `\"a\"` to add to the end. **In Excel:** Save As",
       "code": "with open(\"notes.txt\", \"w\") as f:\n    f.write(\"line one\\n\")\n    f.write(\"line two\\n\")\nprint(\"saved\")"
      },
      {
       "name": "file.read() for line in file:",
       "say": "Read the whole file as one text, or go through it one line at a time. **Watch for:** Lines end with a hidden new-line character; `.strip()` removes it. **In Excel:** File > Open",
       "code": "with open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    text = f.read()\nprint(text)\nwith open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    for line in f:\n        print(line.strip())"
      },
      {
       "name": "open(path, \"a\")",
       "say": "Append mode: add to the end of a file without erasing it.",
       "code": "with open(\"notes.txt\", \"a\") as f:\n    f.write(\"line three\\n\")\nwith open(\"notes.txt\") as f:\n    print(len(f.readlines()), \"lines\")"
      },
      {
       "name": "json.dumps(x) json.loads(text)",
       "say": "JSON is the common text format for dictionaries and lists. `dumps` turns Python data into text; `loads` turns text back. **Watch for:** JSON needs double quotes and has no tuples; `True` becomes `true`.",
       "code": "import json\nrecord = {\"name\": \"Asha\", \"scores\": [70, 85]}\ntext = json.dumps(record)\nprint(text, type(text))\nback = json.loads(text)\nprint(back[\"scores\"], type(back))"
      },
      {
       "name": "json.dump(x, file) json.load(file)",
       "say": "The same, straight to and from an open file. **In Excel:** Save as .json",
       "code": "import json\ndata = {\"city\": \"Pune\", \"visits\": [3, 5]}\nwith open(\"data.json\", \"w\") as f:\n    json.dump(data, f)\nwith open(\"data.json\") as f:\n    print(json.load(f))"
      },
      {
       "name": "csv module csv.reader / csv.DictReader",
       "say": "Read comma-separated rows. `DictReader` gives each row as a dictionary keyed by the header row. (Later, pandas does this in one line.) **Watch for:** Every value is text; convert numbers yourself. **In Excel:** File > Open a .csv",
       "code": "import csv, io\ntext = \"name,score\\nAsha,87\\nBen,72\\n\"\nfor row in csv.DictReader(io.StringIO(text)):\n    print(row[\"name\"], int(row[\"score\"]))"
      },
      {
       "name": "try: ... except ErrorType:",
       "say": "Try something risky; if that kind of error happens, run the fallback instead of crashing. **Watch for:** Name the error you expect. A bare `except:` hides every bug, including typos. **In Excel:** =IFERROR(x, fallback)",
       "code": "values = [\"4\", \"x\", \"5\"]\ntotal = 0\nfor v in values:\n    try:\n        total += int(v)\n    except ValueError:\n        print(\"skipping\", v)\nprint(total)"
      },
      {
       "name": "except Exception as e / finally",
       "say": "`as e` gives you the error object to print. `finally` runs whatever happens (tidy-up).",
       "code": "try:\n    result = 10 / 0\nexcept ZeroDivisionError as e:\n    print(\"Problem:\", e)\nfinally:\n    print(\"always runs\")"
      },
      {
       "name": "raise ValueError(\"message\") assert test",
       "say": "`raise` stops with your own error; `assert` stops if a test is False. Both guard against bad data early.",
       "code": "def percent(part, whole):\n    if whole == 0:\n        raise ValueError(\"whole must not be 0\")\n    return part / whole * 100\n\ntry:\n    percent(5, 0)\nexcept ValueError as e:\n    print(e)\nassert percent(1, 4) == 25.0\nprint(\"checks passed\")"
      },
      {
       "name": "import x from x import y import x as z",
       "say": "Bring in a toolbox. `as` gives it a shorter nickname (you will see `import numpy as np` everywhere). **Watch for:** Only import what you use; names from `from x import y` are used without the prefix.",
       "code": "import math\nfrom math import sqrt\nimport statistics as st\nprint(math.pi, sqrt(49), st.mean([2, 4, 9]))"
      },
      {
       "name": "statistics.mean / median / stdev",
       "say": "Quick statistics for a list: the average, the middle value and the spread. **Watch for:** `median` is the middle after sorting: it ignores extreme values. **In Excel:** =AVERAGE() =MEDIAN() =STDEV()",
       "code": "import statistics as st\nmarks = [70, 85, 60, 90, 55]\nprint(st.mean(marks))\nprint(st.median(marks))\nprint(round(st.stdev(marks), 2))"
      },
      {
       "name": "collections.Counter(list)",
       "say": "Counts how many times each item appears, in one line, and can list the most common. **Watch for:** A Counter behaves like a dictionary. **In Excel:** a pivot table of counts",
       "code": "from collections import Counter\nsales = [\"tea\", \"coffee\", \"tea\", \"tea\", \"coffee\", \"juice\"]\ncounts = Counter(sales)\nprint(counts)\nprint(counts.most_common(2))"
      },
      {
       "name": "collections.defaultdict(list)",
       "say": "A dictionary that creates a missing entry for you: perfect for grouping. **In Excel:** a pivot table: group and total",
       "code": "from collections import defaultdict\nsales = [(\"Pune\", 120), (\"Delhi\", 80), (\"Pune\", 60)]\nby_city = defaultdict(list)\nfor city, amount in sales:\n    by_city[city].append(amount)\nprint(dict(by_city))\nprint({c: sum(v) for c, v in by_city.items()})"
      },
      {
       "name": "datetime.date timedelta strftime",
       "say": "Work with dates: make one, add days, show it the way you like. **Watch for:** `weekday()` gives 0 for Monday up to 6 for Sunday. **In Excel:** =DATE() =TODAY() =TEXT(date, \"dd mmm yyyy\")",
       "code": "from datetime import date, timedelta\nstart = date(2024, 3, 15)\nprint(start.strftime(\"%d %b %Y\"))\nprint(start + timedelta(days=30))\nprint((date(2024, 12, 25) - start).days, \"days to go\")\nprint(start.weekday())"
      },
      {
       "name": "random.seed / random.random / random.choice / random.randint",
       "say": "Make random numbers. Setting a **seed** first makes the \"random\" sequence repeat, so your results are reproducible. **Watch for:** Always set a seed when you want to share or repeat a result. **In Excel:** =RAND() =RANDBETWEEN(1,6)",
       "code": "import random\nrandom.seed(42)\nprint(random.random())\nprint(random.randint(1, 6))\nprint(random.choice([\"tea\", \"coffee\", \"juice\"]))\nprint(random.sample(range(1, 50), 3))"
      },
      {
       "name": "os.path.exists(path) os.listdir()",
       "say": "Does a file exist? What files are here?",
       "code": "import os\nprint(os.path.exists(\"notes.txt\"))\nprint(os.path.exists(\"nothing.txt\"))\nprint(sorted(os.listdir(\".\")))"
      }
     ]
    }
   ]
  }
